%N A301593 a(n) = k if n can be written as the sum of the factorials of k distinct positive integers. Otherwise, a(n) = 0.
%C A301593 The representation of n, when it exists, is unique up to ordering. See the Wikipedia article "Factorial number system" for a proof. - _Adam Reichert_, Oct 22 2026
%C A301593 a(n) > 0 iff every digit in the factorial number system representation of n is 0 or 1. In that case, a(n) is the number of 1's. - _Adam Reichert_, Oct 22 2026
%H A301593 Seiichi Manyama, <a href="/A301593/b301593.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>.
%H A301593 Adam Reichert, <a href="/A301593/a301593.txt">Python program</a>.
%H A301593 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factorial_number_system">Factorial number system</a>.
%F A301593 a(n!) = 1, a(n!+1) = 2 for n >= 2. [Corrected by _Adam Reichert_, Oct 22 2026]
%e A301593 n |                        | a(n)
%e A301593 --+------------------------+-----
%e A301593 1 | 1!                     |  1
%e A301593 2 | 2!                     |  1
%e A301593 3 | 1! + 2!                |  2
%e A301593 6 | 3!                     |  1
%e A301593 7 | 3! + 1!                |  2
%e A301593 8 | 3! + 2!                |  2
%e A301593 9 | 3! + 2! + 1!           |  3
